<p><!-- /oc-video-wrapper --></p>
<p>The greater physical freedom of the nurses’ uniforms extended to ordinary young women as well. Their underwear—a midriff-baring chemise, knickers and petticoat—allowed for easier movement, as shorter skirts led to glamorous stockings and—gasp!—shaved legs!</p>
<p>Trendy cardigans, jumpers and waistcoats weren’t just cute, they helped make up for the lost warmth of those oh-so-restrictive corsets.</p>
<p>View more of <a href="https://www.patreon.com/crowseye">Crow’s Eye Productions</a>’ short films on the history of dress <a href="https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCb1odBsfUVstnlaYW_IiHWw">here</a>.</p>
<p>Knitters, you can find over 70 patterns for WW1 comforts and necessities in the book <em><a href="https://www.amazon.com/Centenary-Stitches-Telling-through-knitting/dp/0993061400">Centenary Stitches: Telling the Story of One WW1 Family Through Vintage Knitting and Crochet</a>.</em></p>
